id "q-279291" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2},$ solve $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}$\n$⋃[$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-2x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}[(x(x-1)-2(x-1)] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=0$, $x=1$ and $x=2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=1$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/baf599e732aa524409881d4bc7598c3f998cffe116ca1cdcd76fb6e6bd49a078.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\in {0}⋃[1,2].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-279291/field-1" "q-279291/field-2" "q-279291/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48de9cc7-5f86-51e4-a827-796f77381af2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-279291"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-87672/selection/905df4df60cbeedb3e2943cabbda767728b15131ac33fc8d800102cc7e249931" :answer/id #uuid "95858e22-d854-5bad-942a-d2a015ea2dfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87672/selection/042597f9bde6403598a7714d62c3c99b0983193d7ee8c266990a112dc1d5e3f8" :answer/id #uuid "c1352168-37f5-5bb1-96d8-a433e558d8b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-3,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87672/selection/69f733c3c7842d081eb7a0ac637f49b