\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-116437/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48de9cc7-5f86-51e4-a827-796f77381af2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116437"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-278843/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "7ce0104d-ecd6-5db4-8aa4-ec358fd85e08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278843/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "879aa35a-b6ed-5487-817f-cfdd2d7df3e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278843/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "edfdcddc-3f2c-5359-be30-4fee91bff8aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278843/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-278843/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-278843/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278843/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "fec0cc6f-044b-5b5d-a0a7-5c582639c70e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278843/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e00ead2-a557-5f1a-afd8-7f7882349b22" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278843/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-278843/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278843" :question/id #uuid "110555f5-945b-5700-926f-9357921178e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-278843" :question/problem "Given $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x$, solve $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$ \n{{field-1}}\n$}\\,⋃\\,[$\n{{field-2}}$,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}+6x+9) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}+3x+3x+9) \\\\  & =x(x(x+3)+3(x+3)) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x+3) \\\\  & =x(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x$ are $x=0$ and $x=-3,$ where\n\n- $x=-3$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=0$ is a simple root.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/24adb4d2477b6db5b51f538f5f3f57c197cbe3d1df53ad1b4e7e9479879f18dd.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in {-3}⋃[0,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-278843/field-1" "q-278843/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48de9cc7-5f86-51e4-a827-796f77381af2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-278843"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-87387/selection/1ae0c3424c40809156346e166d8d530b2e2d9863c489751434298415aba42372" :answer/id #uuid "9bb7a87d-90e6-5de6-a01e-e5f0679e55a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [-1,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87387/selection/308265115b2167733f6470bb0d9c54501713bd0f9f28f39ae4fc1cbea5bb96f8" :answer/id #uuid "2fefabe4-624f-549b-aa21-8bf074067925" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [0,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87387/selection/5eb6157e768e02b44b13658d75d2ae31c48af37d1d9f0c53dabf5862f8f171ef" :answer/id #uuid "498dc4e1-cd80-52e3-9375-c1f2561334cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup {0}\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87387/selection/2683037ae2be212729599f254edb839ea177c6723825b60055d1b06fae9b7134" :answer/id #uuid "2d200c3a-c7b6-5490-b6f2-a7fc61ef651d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87387/selection/edd686d8fa8997e9a127a517a739a32eae58458d3bcf31cfd36b5a97c96d9a37" :answer/id #uuid "b348ede3-4bb7-57d1-9459-015730f7a9a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [-1,0]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87387/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d28fb417-3330-5704-a32e-b53ff423b07b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-87387/selection/5eb6157e768e02b44b13658d75d2ae31c48af37d1d9f0c53dabf5862f8f171ef" :answer-field/choices ["q-87387/selection/1ae0c3424c40809156346e166d8d530b2e2d9863c489751434298415aba42372" "q-87387/selection/308265115b2167733f6470bb0d9c54501713bd0f9f28f39ae4fc1cbea5bb96f8" "q-87387/selection/5eb6157e768e02b44b13658d75d2ae31c48af37d1d9f0c53dabf5862f8f171ef" "q-87387/selection/2683037ae2be212729599f254edb839ea177c6723825b60055d1b06fae9b7134" "q-87387/selection/edd686d8fa8997e9a127a517a739a32eae58458d3bcf31cfd36b5a97c96d9a37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-87387" :question/id #uuid "5f8f98c2-651c-5070-b539-61b041780f42" :question/math-academy-id "q-87387" :question/problem "If $P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{2},$ solve $P(x)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $P(x)\\ge 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+1)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{2}$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1,$  where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/e6aa75d55f2776e3e2b5d029ad51ad9e39a80e49b613705a97fa01ff34113db5.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)\\ge 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup {0}\\cup [1,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-87387/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48de9cc7-5f86-51e4-a827-796f77381af2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-87387"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5819"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/55018a35718d655dc2b0d67692768e7c78ee9c064f137b31435a204280734c08.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of the polynomial $y = p(x)$ shown above, find the solution to $p(x) < 0$." :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $p(x) <    0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis. These intervals are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/141519a0d0abc0d1545d940aba1fd52b15e96d81b0c10560960df2710da4937f.png)\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (-3, -1) \\cup (0,2).\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that this is a strict inequality, so we do not include the roots in our solution." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5821"] :question/problem "Given the polynomial $P(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2),$ find the solution of $P(x) \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $P(x) \\geq 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2)$ are $x=0$ and $x= \\pm{2},$ where\n\n- $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2,$\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $3,$ and\n\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/435846c9b0efa313b76b0e13689fb2225209d7ee1ea52e332a65bd676d97cc8f.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x) \\geq 0,$ we look for the parts of the curve that are above the $x$-axis or touching the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,0] \\cup [2, \\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5829"] :question/problem "Given $f(x) = x^3 - 9x,$ solve $f(x) \\gt 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $f(x) \\gt 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y = f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}-9x \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - 9x$ are $x= - 3,$ $x=0,$ and $x = 3,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/91e0e81b98e7f89796c277854bec6ce0240c797fc0c3d1eda3f33ce4bc9c85a6.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx \\in (-3,0) \\cup (3,\\infty).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
